---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Pricing &amp; insurance — DuBose, M.D." active="pricing" bookbar={true}>

  <!-- 1 · Intro -->
  <section>
    <div class="narrow">
      <h1>Pricing</h1>
      <p style="margin-top:20px">
        One number, explained completely. If anything here is unclear, ask me
        directly — that is what the free conversation is for.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 · The price card -->
  <section class="band-warm">
    <div class="wrap" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center">
      <div class="price-card">
        <div class="amt">$100 <span>per month</span></div>
        <div class="per">per adult patient</div>
        <p class="small" style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:10px">Included, with no per-visit charges:</p>
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Visits as your care needs them — video, home when clinically
          appropriate, clinic space when needed</li>
          <li>Direct messaging with me, answered in 1&ndash;2 business days</li>
          <li>Every result reviewed and explained</li>
          <li>Prescriptions managed; refills handled without games</li>
          <li>Referrals and coordination across your other doctors</li>
          <li>Forms and paperwork handled — disability, FMLA, insurance
          forms, letters, prior authorizations</li>
          <li>An unhurried annual review of everything</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:20px">
          No enrollment fee. No contract. No commitment. Cancel anytime,
          effective at the end of your billing month. Monthly by card or bank
          account; annual prepayment available at the same price.
        </p>
        <a class="btn" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a>
      </div>
      <p class="small soft" style="margin-top:20px;text-align:center">
        Each adult enrolls individually: a couple is $200, three adult family
        members $300. I care for adults only.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 · Access rate -->
  <section>
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>If $100 a month is a real barrier</h2>
      <p>
        I keep a limited number of reduced-rate memberships for people for
        whom the standard price would genuinely prevent care. There is no
        application form and no means test — it is a conversation, and I keep
        simple written criteria so it is fair. If cost is the thing stopping
        you, say so when we talk.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 · What the fee does not cover -->
  <section class="band-warm">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What the fee does not cover — and how those get paid</h2>
      <p>
        My fee covers my work as your physician. Care delivered by others is
        billed by them, usually through your insurance:
      </p>
      <div class="pay-rows">
        <div class="row">
          <b>Labs</b>
          <p>Through your insurance — or, where I can arrange discounted
          direct pricing, passed to you at my cost, told to you before
          anything is drawn. Often a fraction of list price.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Imaging</b>
          <p>Through your insurance; I order what is needed and explain the
          results.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Medications</b>
          <p>Your pharmacy and insurance as now. I help you find fair
          prices; Texas law does not allow physicians to sell medications.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Specialists</b>
          <p>Through your insurance; I refer carefully and stay involved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Urgent care, ER, hospital</b>
          <p>Through your insurance, as today. My fee never replaces
          coverage for these.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:24px">
        This is why I recommend every member keep health coverage: the
        membership works alongside insurance, not instead of it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 · How your coverage fits -->
  <section id="bookbar-after">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How your coverage fits</h2>
      <p>
        Whatever insurance you have — or none — you can join. The price and
        the care are the same for everyone. Government programs add one
        signature:
      </p>
      <div class="pay-rows">
        <div class="row">
          <b>Private insurance or none</b>
          <p>Sign up. That is all.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Medicare</b>
          <p>One additional document — a Medicare private contract — is
          required by federal rules. Your Medicare continues to pay for labs,
          imaging, specialists, prescriptions, and hospital care exactly as
          before. My fee covers only my services.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Medicare Advantage</b>
          <p>Same private contract. If your plan is an HMO, we will talk
          through how your plan handles tests I order before you join.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Texas Medicaid</b>
          <p>A short private-pay acknowledgment is required by state rules.
          Your Medicaid continues to work outside our relationship; if you
          have traditional (non-plan) Medicaid, we will discuss how orders
          are covered first.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:24px">
        The one exception: federal rules do not permit private arrangements
        with patients in certain combined Medicare and Medicaid programs (QMB
        and similar). If that is your situation, I will help you find good
        care instead.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        Using HSA funds: recent federal law may allow it for memberships like
        this one. Confirm with your tax advisor.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 · Why $100, honestly -->
  <section class="band-warm">
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>Why $100, honestly</h2>
      <p>
        Concierge medicine typically charges $2,400 to $5,000 a year in
        membership fees — and usually still bills your insurance for visits.
        A single cash-pay office visit commonly runs $130 to $200. Direct
        primary care practices like this one average about $100 a month
        nationally.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        I can charge this because the practice is small by design: no office
        building, no billing department, no volume targets — a limited number
        of patients, each of whom I actually know. The price is not
        subsidized by anything. It is what unhurried primary care costs when
        the overhead is honest.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 · What's the catch? -->
  <section>
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>What&rsquo;s the catch?</h2>
      <p>
        There are limits, and they are stated rather than hidden: I keep
        business hours and am not an emergency service. I take real time away
        each year. And when my panel is full, it is full; I would rather
        close enrollment than dilute attention. That is the whole catch.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:22px">
        <a class="quiet" href="/membership-agreement.pdf">Read the full membership agreement (PDF)</a>
        — yes, before you ever talk to me.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8 · CTA -->
  <section class="band-tint">
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>Questions about your specific situation?</h2>
      <p>
        Thirty minutes, by video, free — bring your coverage questions and I
        will answer them plainly.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:26px"><a class="btn" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
